* Morning watch (07:51)  The pilot, Mr Tanguy of Brest, came aboard from the cutter to pilot her in (American colours being no bar at Brest).
* Morning watch (07:51)  The pilot says: Range along either shore of the Goulet and fear nothing, but not the middle, for the Fillettes and the Mingan lie there; the passage between them is very dangerous for the rocks under water. Keep the castle of Brest in full view, clear of Penaleuch point, and you are in the south channel. When Point Bertheaume bears north you may stand to the east for the Goulet. You are upon the Fillettes when the two steep points east of Camaret come in one and the castle is hid by Penaleuch point; the Mingan shows above water except at high water springs. Within the Bay of Brest you anchor where you please in eight, ten or sixteen fathoms on mud; the ships of the line moor in the Penfeld under the castle. Without the Goulet the road of Bertheaume has eight to twelve fathoms on sand and mud, sheltered from the north-west round to north-east, and Camaret road eight to ten on mud, safe from the south. High water at Brest about half past four in the afternoon, the tide rising some 21 feet; the flood will serve from about eleven o'clock in the morning.
  Morning watch (07:51)  The pilot's news: Britain at war with the Batavian Republic, France and Spain.
  Morning watch (07:51)  A letter from the agent of the owners came aboard by the pilot cutter; Ebenr. Schley took it aft for the captain, who is on the quarterdeck.
  Morning watch (07:51)  Standing orders 'the pilot boards' and 'the pilot aboard' (both the captain's) give contrary orders on the fore topsail; the later stands.
  Morning watch (07:51)  Standing order 'the pilot aboard': order not carried out ('set plain sail'): Could not set plain sail: 'set the plain sail': Nothing done: the fore sail is already set; the main sail is already set; the fore topsail is already set; the fore topgallant is already set; the fore staysail is already set; the jib is already set.
